thDecomposition_width_eq G\nIntervalThickness.lean-259-  have hne : {k | ∃ H : SimpleGraph V, G ≤ H ∧ IsIntervalGraph H ∧ H.cliqueNum = k}.Nonempty :=\n--\nIntervalThickness.lean:267:theorem intervalThickness_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one [Nonempty V] (G : SimpleGraph V)\nIntervalThickness.lean-268-    [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\nIntervalThickness.lean-269-    intervalThickness G = vertexSeparation G + 1 := by\nIntervalThickness.lean-270-  rw [intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one, vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth]\nIntervalThickness.lean-271-\n--\nIntervalThickness.lean:273:theorem intervalThickness_of_isEmpty [IsEmpty V] (G : SimpleGraph V) :\nIntervalThickness.lean-274-    intervalThickness G = 0 := by\nIntervalThickness.lean-275-  obtain ⟨D, -⟩ := exists_pathDecomposition_width_eq G\nIntervalThickness.lean-276-  exact Nat.eq_zero_of_le_zero <|\nIntervalThickness.lean-277-    Nat.sInf_le ⟨bagGraph D, le_bagGraph D, isIntervalGraph_bagGraph D,\n/-\nCopyright (c) 2026 Alexandre Linhares. All rights reserved.\nReleased under the MIT license as described in the file LICENSE.\n\n# Node search, monotone: `mns = vs + 1`\n\nTable 1 of Linhares & Yanasse (2002), row \"node search game\", source [9]\nKirousis & Papadimitriou (1985), *Interval graphs and searching*, Discrete\nMathematics 55, 181–184; the relation to vertex separation is [10] Kirousis &\nPapadimitriou (1986), *Searching and pebbling*, Theoretical Computer Science\n47, 205–218, Theorem 4.1.\n\n## The source's definition\n\n[9] p. 181: \"A searching strategy S is a sequence of moves where the player\neither places a searcher on a node of the graph that carries no searcher or\ndeletes the searcher of a guarded node. The edges of the graph are initially\nconsidered contaminated by a gas. The object of a searching strategy is to\nclear all edges. The clearing of an edge is accomplished once both its\nendpoints concurrently carry a searcher. A clear edge may be recontaminated\nonce there appears a path that carries no searchers and that connects this\nedge with a contaminated one.\" The node search number `ns(G)` is the least\nmaximum number of searchers of a strategy clearing all edges (p. 182; the same\nin [10] §2, p. 208). A strategy is *recontamination-free* (monotone) if no\nmove recontaminates an edge ([10] §2, `pns`).\n\nFormalised as the game itself, not as a formula:\n\n* `SearchState` — the guarded nodes and the contaminated edges;\n* `SearchMove` — `place v` or `remove v`;\n* `searchStep` — the guard set changes, every edge with both endpoints guarded\n  is cleared, and then every edge joined to a still-contaminated edge by a\n  searcher-free path (`FreeReach`: a path all of whose vertices are unguarded,\n  from an endpoint of the one to an endpoint of the other) is contaminated\n  (`recontaminate`). This is the same semantics as `paper2/complex_check.py`'s\n  `node_search`;\n* `searchCost` — the largest number of searchers over the states of the run;\n* `NoRecontamination` — no move enlarges the contaminated set;\n* `nodeSearch` (all strategies) and `monotoneNodeSearch` (recontamination-free\n  strategies), each the least cost of a strategy that clears every edge.\n\nA move that the source forbids — placing on a guarded node, removing from an\nunguarded one — leaves the guard set as it is; allowing it changes no value,\nbecause on the states a run reaches the step it makes is the identity on the\nguards and cannot lower the cost.\n\nNothing in these definitions mentions layouts, separation or decompositions.\n\n## What is proved\n\n* `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one` — **[10] Theorem 4.1 for\n  the monotone game**: for every graph with at least one edge,\n  `mns(G) = vs(G) + 1`; hence `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n  - `≤`: the shack process of Kornai & Tuza (item 04) is a monotone strategy:\n    place `vᵢ`, then remove every vertex with no neighbour after `vᵢ`\n    (`shackStrategy`). It clears every edge, never recontaminates, and its\n    largest guard set is the shack, so it costs `ν(σ)`\n    (`shackStrategy_isMonotone`), and `ν(σ) = vs(σ reversed) + 1`.\n  - `≥`: from a monotone strategy, order the vertices by the time `τ(v)` from\n    which no contaminated edge touches `v`. At the time `t = τ(vᵢ)` every\n    vertex after position `i` with a neighbour at or before it carries a\n    searcher — either its last contaminated edge was cleared at `t`, or it\n    still has a contaminated edge and an unguarded endpoint would spread the\n    gas back onto the clear edge to its earlier neighbour — and so does `vᵢ`\n    itself, whose last contaminated edge was cleared at `t`\n    (`vertexSeparation_add_one_le_of_monotone`). [10] p. 217 orders instead\n    by the time a node *first accepts* a searcher and counts earlier nodes\n    with a later neighbour, claiming they all carry searchers (its (2)). A\n    monotone strategy may guard a node and delete the searcher before any of\n    its edges is clear, and then the claim fails: on `K_{1,3}`, placing and\n    deleting each leaf in turn and then searching from the centre costs 2, but\n    first acceptance orders the leaves before the centre, and three leaves\n    precede their neighbour. Deleting such useless placements repairs [10]'s\n    argument; ordering by clearing time does not need the repair.\n* Edge case: on an edgeless graph `mns = ns = 0` (the empty strategy), while\n  `vs + 1 = 1` whenever there is a vertex (`monotoneNodeSearch_of_edgeless`,\n  `nodeSearch_of_edgeless`). This is the exception recorded by item 01.\n* `nodeSearch_le_monotoneNodeSearch` — the trivial half of monotonicity, so\n  `ns ≤ vs + 1` for every graph with an edge.\n\n## Not proved: the full game\n\nThat recontamination does not help, `ns = mns` ([10] Theorem 2.3, from\nLaPaugh's theorem for edge search, [10] Theorem 2.1), is stated as the\nproposition `NodeSearchMonotonicity` and not asserted. With it,\n`vs + 1 ≤ ns` follows from the theorem proved here. It is recorded in\n`paper2/equivalences.md` as a named gap; no `sorry` stands for it.\n-/\n\nimport MOSPFormalization.Complex.EdgeSeparation","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"236600cf-c538-4743-9121-10f54d3c9259","uuid":"fe55ad66-6a39-4f37-95af-b8202543e520","timestamp":"2026-09-30T13:30:19.045Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"NodeSearch.lean:380:theorem vertexSeparation_add_one_le_of_monotone {u₀ v₀ : V} (h₀ : G.Adj u₀ v₀) {k : ℕ}\nNodeSearch.lean-381-    {ms : List (SearchMove V)} (h : IsMonotoneNodeSearch G k ms) :\nNodeSearch.lean-382-    vertexSeparation G + 1 ≤ k := by\nNodeSearch.lean-383-  obtain ⟨⟨hclr, hcost⟩, hmono⟩ := h\nNodeSearch.lean-384-  set L := ms.length with hL\n--\nNodeSearch.lean:688:theorem monotoneNodeSearch_le_inNarrowness (σ : LinearLayout V) :\nNodeSearch.lean-689-    monotoneNodeSearch G ≤ inNarrowness G σ :=\nNodeSearch.lean-690-  Nat.sInf_le ⟨shackStrategy G σ, shackStrategy_isMonotone G σ⟩\nNodeSearch.lean-691-\nNodeSearch.lean-692-/-- **Kirousis & Papadimitriou (1986), Theorem 4.1, for the monotone game.**\n--\nNodeSearch.lean:694:theorem monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one {u₀ v₀ : V} (h₀ : G.Adj u₀ v₀) :\nNodeSearch.lean-695-    monotoneNodeSearch G = vertexSeparation G + 1 := by\nNodeSearch.lean-696-  have : Nonempty V := ⟨u₀⟩\nNodeSearch.lean-697-  apply le_antisymm\nNodeSearch.lean-698-  · obtain ⟨σ₀, hσ₀⟩ := exists_layout_vertexSeparation G\n--\nNodeSearch.lean:706:theorem monotoneNodeSearch_eq_pathwidth_add_one {u₀ v₀ : V} (h₀ : G.Adj u₀ v₀) :\nNodeSearch.lean-707-    monotoneNodeSearch G = pathwidth G + 1 := by\nNodeSearch.lean-708-  rw [monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one G h₀, vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth]\nNodeSearch.lean-709-\nNodeSearch.lean-710-/-- A monotone strategy is a strategy. -/\nNodeSearch.lean:711:theorem nodeSearch_le_monotoneNodeSearch : nodeSearch G ≤ monotoneNodeSearch G := by\nNodeSearch.lean-712-  obtain ⟨ms, hms⟩ := Nat.sInf_mem (s := {k | ∃ ms, IsMonotoneNodeSearch G k ms})\nNodeSearch.lean-713-    ⟨_, shackStrategy G (Fintype.equivFin V), shackStrategy_isMonotone G _⟩\nNodeSearch.lean-714-  exact Nat.sInf_le ⟨ms, hms.1⟩\nNodeSearch.lean-715-\n--\nNodeSearch.lean:717:theorem nodeSearch_le_vertexSeparation_add_one {u₀ v₀ : V} (h₀ : G.Adj u₀ v₀) :\nNodeSearch.lean-718-    nodeSearch G ≤ vertexSeparation G + 1 :=\nNodeSearch.lean-719-  (nodeSearch_le_monotoneNodeSearch G).trans\nNodeSearch.lean-720-    (monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one G h₀).le\nNodeSearch.lean-721-\n--\nNodeSearch.lean:723:theorem nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_monotonicity\nNodeSearch.lean-724-    (hmono : NodeSearchMonotonicity G) {u₀ v₀ : V} (h₀ : G.Adj u₀ v₀) :\nNodeSearch.lean-725-    nodeSearch G = vertexSeparation G + 1 := by\nNodeSearch.lean-726-  rw [hmono, monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one G h₀]\nNodeSearch.lean-727-\n--\nNodeSearch.lean:732:theorem monotoneNodeSearch_of_edgeless (h : ∀ u v, ¬ G.Adj u v) :\nNodeSearch.lean-733-    monotoneNodeSearch G = 0 := by\nNodeSearch.lean-734-  apply Nat.eq_zero_of_le_zero\nNodeSearch.lean-735-  refine Nat.sInf_le ⟨[], ⟨?_, ?_⟩, trivial⟩\nNodeSearch.lean-736-  · ext e\n--\nNodeSearch.lean:741:theorem nodeSearch_of_edgeless (h : ∀ u v, ¬ G.Adj u v) : nodeSearch G = 0 :=\nNodeSearch.lean-742-  Nat.eq_zero_of_le_zero ((nodeSearch_le_monotoneNodeSearch G).trans\nNodeSearch.lean-743-    (monotoneNodeSearch_of_edgeless G h).le)\nNodeSearch.lean-744-\nNodeSearch.lean-745-/-- The exception is real: on a nonempty edgeless graph `mns = 0 ≠ vs + 1`. -/\nNodeSearch.lean:746:theorem monotoneNodeSearch_ne_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_edgeless [Nonempty V]\nNodeSearch.lean-747-    (h : ∀ u v, ¬ G.Adj u v) : monotoneNodeSearch G ≠ vertexSeparation G + 1 := by\nNodeSearch.lean-748-  rw [monotoneNodeSearch_of_edgeless G h]\nNodeSearch.lean-749-  omega\nNodeSearch.lean-750-\nIntervalThickness.lean:255:theorem intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one [Nonempty V] (G : SimpleGraph V) :\nIntervalThickness.lean-256-    intervalThickness G = pathwidth G + 1 := by\nIntervalThickness.lean-257-  refine le_antisymm (intervalThickness_le_pathwidth_add_one G) ?_\nIntervalThickness.lean-258-  obtain ⟨D, -⟩ := exists_pathDecomposition_width_eq G\nIntervalThickness.lean-259-  have hne : {k | ∃ H : SimpleGraph V, G ≤ H ∧ IsIntervalGraph H ∧ H.cliqueNum = k}.Nonempty :=\n--\nIntervalThickness.lean:267:theorem intervalThickness_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one [Nonempty V] (G : SimpleGraph V)\nIntervalThickness.lean-268-    [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\nIntervalThickness.lean-269-    intervalThickness G = vertexSeparation G + 1 := by\nIntervalThickness.lean-270-  rw [intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one, vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth]\nIntervalThickness.lean-271-\n--\nIntervalThickness.lean:273:theorem intervalThickness_of_isEmpty [IsEmpty V] (G : SimpleGraph V) :\nIntervalThickness.lean-274-    intervalThickness G = 0 := by\nIntervalThickness.lean-275-  obtain ⟨D, -⟩ := exists_pathDecomposition_width_eq G\nIntervalThickness.lean-276-  exact Nat.eq_zero_of_le_zero <|\nIntervalThickness.lean-277-    Nat.sInf_le ⟨bagGraph D, le_bagGraph D, isIntervalGraph_bagGraph D,\n/-\nCopyright (c) 2026 Alexandre Linhares. All rights reserved.\nReleased under the MIT license as described in the file LICENSE.\n\n# Node search, monotone: `mns = vs + 1`\n\nTable 1 of Linhares & Yanasse (2002), row \"node search game\", source [9]\nKirousis & Papadimitriou (1985), *Interval graphs and searching*, Discrete\nMathematics 55, 181–184; the relation to vertex separation is [10] Kirousis &\nPapadimitriou (1986), *Searching and pebbling*, Theoretical Computer Science\n47, 205–218, Theorem 4.1.\n\n## The source's definition\n\n[9] p. 181: \"A searching strategy S is a sequence of moves where the player\neither places a searcher on a node of the graph that carries no searcher or\ndeletes the searcher of a guarded node. The edges of the graph are initially\nconsidered contaminated by a gas. The object of a searching strategy is to\nclear all edges. The clearing of an edge is accomplished once both its\nendpoints concurrently carry a searcher. A clear edge may be recontaminated\nonce there appears a path that carries no searchers and that connects this\nedge with a contaminated one.\" The node search number `ns(G)` is the least\nmaximum number of searchers of a strategy clearing all edges (p. 182; the same\nin [10] §2, p. 208). A strategy is *recontamination-free* (monotone) if no\nmove recontaminates an edge ([10] §2, `pns`).\n\nFormalised as the game itself, not as a formula:\n\n* `SearchState` — the guarded nodes and the contaminated edges;\n* `SearchMove` — `place v` or `remove v`;\n* `searchStep` — the guard set changes, every edge with both endpoints guarded\n  is cleared, and then every edge joined to a still-contaminated edge by a\n  searcher-free path (`FreeReach`: a path all of whose vertices are unguarded,\n  from an endpoint of the one to an endpoint of the other) is contaminated\n  (`recontaminate`). This is the same semantics as `paper2/complex_check.py`'s\n  `node_search`;\n* `searchCost` — the largest number of searchers over the states of the run;\n* `NoRecontamination` — no move enlarges the contaminated set;\n* `nodeSearch` (all strategies) and `monotoneNodeSearch` (recontamination-free\n  strategies), each the least cost of a strategy that clears every edge.\n\nA move that the source forbids — placing on a guarded node, removing from an\nunguarded one — leaves the guard set as it is; allowing it changes no value,\nbecause on the states a run reaches the step it makes is the identity on the\nguards and cannot lower the cost.\n\nNothing in these definitions mentions layouts, separation or decompositions.\n\n## What is proved\n\n* `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one` — **[10] Theorem 4.1 for\n  the monotone game**: for every graph with at least one edge,\n  `mns(G) = vs(G) + 1`; hence `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n  - `≤`: the shack process of Kornai & Tuza (item 04) is a monotone strategy:\n    place `vᵢ`, then remove every vertex with no neighbour after `vᵢ`\n    (`shackStrategy`). It clears every edge, never recontaminates, and its\n    largest guard set is the shack, so it costs `ν(σ)`\n    (`shackStrategy_isMonotone`), and `ν(σ) = vs(σ reversed) + 1`.\n  - `≥`: from a monotone strategy, order the vertices by the time `τ(v)` from\n    which no contaminated edge touches `v`. At the time `t = τ(vᵢ)` every\n    vertex after position `i` with a neighbour at or before it carries a\n    searcher — either its last contaminated edge was cleared at `t`, or it\n    still has a contaminated edge and an unguarded endpoint would spread the\n    gas back onto the clear edge to its earlier neighbour — and so does `vᵢ`\n    itself, whose last contaminated edge was cleared at `t`\n    (`vertexSeparation_add_one_le_of_monotone`). [10] p. 217 orders instead\n    by the time a node *first accepts* a searcher and counts earlier nodes\n    with a later neighbour, claiming they all carry searchers (its (2)). A\n    monotone strategy may guard a node and delete the searcher before any of\n    its edges is clear, and then the claim fails: on `K_{1,3}`, placing and\n    deleting each leaf in turn and then searching from the centre costs 2, but\n    first acceptance orders the leaves before the centre, and three leaves\n    precede their neighbour. Deleting such useless placements repairs [10]'s\n    argument; ordering by clearing time does not need the repair.\n* Edge case: on an edgeless graph `mns = ns = 0` (the empty strategy), while\n  `vs + 1 = 1` whenever there is a vertex (`monotoneNodeSearch_of_edgeless`,\n  `nodeSearch_of_edgeless`). This is the exception recorded by item 01.\n* `nodeSearch_le_monotoneNodeSearch` — the trivial half of monotonicity, so\n  `ns ≤ vs + 1` for every graph with an edge.\n\n## Not proved: the full game\n\nThat recontamination does not help, `ns = mns` ([10] Theorem 2.3, from\nLaPaugh's theorem for edge search, [10] Theorem 2.1), is stated as the\nproposition `NodeSearchMonotonicity` and not asserted. With it,\n`vs + 1 ≤ ns` follows from the theorem proved here. It is recorded in\n`paper2/equivalences.md` as a named gap; no `sorry` stands for it.\n-/\n\nimport MOSPFormalization.Complex.EdgeSeparation","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
