{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZiycH7nJBZfDdkqWbms","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 12 is done. The new Lean file is `lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/IntervalSearch.lean`, imported from the root. It builds with no `sorry`; its main theorems depend only on the axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`. The gate passes (lake build, 1,231 tests).\n\n**Proved, for graphs with at least one edge:**\n- **Kirousis & Papadimitriou (1985)'s theorem for the monotone game** (`monotoneNodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness`): monotone node search number = interval thickness.\n- **The whole chain in one statement** (`intervalSearch_chain`): interval thickness = monotone node search = vertex separation + 1 = pathwidth + 1.\n- **Each direction for a single object:**\n  - any interval supergraph `H` gives monotone node search ≤ the clique number of `H` (`monotoneNodeSearch_le_cliqueNum`);\n  - any monotone strategy of cost `k` gives interval thickness ≤ `k` (`intervalThickness_le_of_isMonotoneNodeSearch`).\n- **The full game (recontamination allowed):** node search ≤ interval thickness is proved outright. Equality is derived only from the named gap `NodeSearchMonotonicity` from item 10, which is stated but not asserted.\n- **Möhring (1990) Theorem 3.9** (gate matrix tracks = node search number of the net graph) for the monotone game, when two nets share a gate (`NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_monotoneNodeSearch`).\n\n**Counterexamples proved in Lean:**\n- Kirousis & Papadimitriou's theorem says \"for any graph G\", but on every nonempty edgeless graph the node search number is 0 and the interval thickness is 1. The smallest case is one vertex (`nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_K1`).\n- Möhring's Theorem 3.9, read literally, fails on the 1 × 1 matrix `[1]`: it needs 1 track, but its net graph has no edges, so its node search number is 0 (`tracks_ne_monotoneNodeSearch_one`).\n- Both values stay within ±1 of the open-stack number, so Table 1's \"±1\" wording still holds on these inputs.\n- Every other claim that items 01–02 found false already had a Lean counterexample from items 08–10. The rows that remain inexact (edge search, split bandwidth, Ohtsuki's boundary variant) have values confined to a band or sandwich around the claimed one, so they are weaker than stated rather than false.\n\nI updated `paper2/equivalences.md` (rows 5 and 6, their census sections, and a new \"Item 12\" section). The Table 1 edit ran just before the gate; one sentence was reworded after it ran. `PROGRESS.md` now reads 12/14, and the item is marked `[x]` in `iterations.md`. Items 13 and 14 are still open, so I did not add `LOOP_COMPLETE`, and nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1255,"cache_read_input_tokens":114873,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1255,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":809,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"236600cf-c538-4743-9121-10f54d3c9259","uuid":"6aa2ca0b-4b7a-4420-a0de-cb8b0f26b9a1","timestamp":"2026-09-30T13:45:02.753Z","request_id":"req_011CfZiybqq77usXYUjEnzLo"}
